export const P1={title:'The patient care technician: role, compliance and professionalism',hook:'Patient care technicians combine the personal care of a nursing assistant with advanced skills—drawing blood, recording EKGs, checking glucose and caring for IV sites and catheters. Hospitals rely on them in emergency departments, medical-surgical units and dialysis centers. Compliance, safety and professional responsibility make up 20% of the test.',objectives:['Describe the Patient Care Technician event, test plan and Round Two skills.','Explain the PCT’s role, scope of practice and certification.','Apply legal, ethical and privacy requirements in patient care.','Communicate professionally with patients and the care team.'],sections:[
['The Patient Care Technician event',[
'Sponsored by the **National Healthcareer Association (NHA)**, Patient Care Technician has a **50-question test in 60 minutes** (no time announcements at ILC) built from NHA’s **PCT SkillsBuilder Plus** and **CPCT/A online study guide**. The test plan: **patient care 44%**, **compliance, safety and professional responsibility 20%**, **phlebotomy 14%**, **infection control 12%** and **EKG 10%**. Patient care is nearly half the test, so vital signs, mobility, personal care, elimination and observation deserve the most study time.',
'Round Two is a written scenario using one or more skills: **I** discontinue a peripheral IV (5 minutes), **II** apply sequential compression devices (6), **III** measure an infant’s length and weight (7), **IV** urinary catheter maintenance (5), **V** routine ETS venipuncture (10), **VI** obtain a 12-lead EKG (10) and **VII** glucometer (8). Competitors bring photo ID, two #2 pencils and barrier devices—**two pairs of non-latex gloves, a gown, goggles or safety glasses and a mask**. A combined Round Two score of **70% or higher** is required for recognition; final rank adds both rounds, with ties broken by the test score.',
match('Match the skill to its time',[['Discontinue peripheral IV','5 minutes'],['Sequential compression devices','6 minutes'],['Infant length and weight','7 minutes'],['Urinary catheter maintenance','5 minutes'],['Routine ETS venipuncture','10 minutes'],['12-lead EKG','10 minutes']])]],
['The PCT role and scope',[
'PCTs work under the supervision of **registered nurses** in hospitals, emergency departments, dialysis units, long-term care and clinics. Duties include **activities of daily living** (bathing, feeding, toileting, mobility), **vital signs** and measurements, **intake and output**, **specimen collection**, **phlebotomy**, **12-lead EKGs** and telemetry support, **point-of-care glucose testing**, **catheter care**, applying **sequential compression devices**, discontinuing **peripheral IVs** where allowed, wound and skin care, and observing and reporting changes. The exact scope depends on **state law, facility policy and training**.',
'PCTs do **not** assess patients the way nurses do, diagnose, administer medications (outside narrow facility-specific exceptions) or start IVs in most settings. When asked to perform a task outside training or policy, a PCT politely declines and notifies the nurse. Certification such as NHA’s **Certified Patient Care Technician/Assistant (CPCT/A)** demonstrates competence, and many states require nurse aide registry listing for the personal-care portion of the role.',
sort('Within the PCT role or not?',['Usually within PCT scope','Outside PCT scope'],[['Performing a 12-lead EKG',0],['Drawing blood by venipuncture',0],['Checking capillary blood glucose',0],['Interpreting the EKG to diagnose a heart attack',1],['Giving an IV medication',1],['Applying sequential compression devices',0],['Prescribing a diet',1],['Reporting a change in vital signs',0]])]],
['Legal and ethical responsibilities',[
'PCTs are accountable for their actions. **Negligence** is failing to provide the care a reasonably prudent PCT would, causing harm; **malpractice** is professional negligence. **Assault** (threat), **battery** (touching without consent), **false imprisonment** (restraining without authority), **invasion of privacy** and **defamation** are intentional torts. **Consent** may be implied (a patient extends an arm for a blood draw) or expressed, and patients may refuse any procedure—refusals are reported to the nurse and documented. Before every skill, HOSA rating sheets require explaining the procedure, and for invasive skills, **obtaining consent**.',
'Ethical principles—**autonomy**, **beneficence**, **nonmaleficence**, **justice**, **fidelity** and **veracity**—guide decisions. **Mandatory reporting** applies to suspected abuse or neglect of children, older adults and vulnerable adults. **Documentation** is a legal record: write objective facts, record times, never document before performing care and correct errors according to policy.',
match('Match the term to the example',[['Battery','Drawing blood after the patient refused'],['Implied consent','Patient holds out an arm for the blood draw'],['Negligence','Failing to lock bed wheels, leading to a fall'],['False imprisonment','Restraining a patient without an order'],['Veracity','Reporting your own charting error honestly'],['Mandatory reporting','Reporting suspected elder abuse']])]],
['Privacy, identification and documentation',[
'**HIPAA** protects patients’ health information. PCTs access only what they need for their tasks, keep screens and paper records out of view, never discuss patients in public areas or on social media and share information only with authorized team members. Patient identification is a major safety requirement: **The Joint Commission’s National Patient Safety Goals** require **at least two identifiers**—HOSA skills specify **checking the ID bracelet and asking the patient to state their name and birthdate**, and for lab work, verbally matching the identification to the requisition.',
'Accurate documentation includes the date and time, what was done, the results and the patient’s response, using approved abbreviations and the 24-hour clock where used. Many HOSA skills end with documenting in the **Nurses’ Notes**, completing the requisition or uploading results, and **reporting abnormal findings to the nurse**.',
chk('Which identification method meets HOSA and patient safety standards?',['Check the ID bracelet and ask the patient to state name and birthdate','Ask, “Are you Mr. Lee?”','Use the room number','Rely on memory from yesterday'],'Use two identifiers and ask open questions.')]],
['Communication and teamwork',[
'PCTs communicate constantly—with patients who may be frightened or in pain, with families and with nurses and providers. Effective communication includes introducing yourself and your role, explaining each procedure in plain language before starting, listening actively, respecting cultural and language needs (using qualified interpreters) and checking understanding. Nonverbal communication—eye contact, tone, posture and gentle touch—builds trust. Every HOSA skill awards points for “appropriate verbal and nonverbal communication.”',
'With the team, PCTs give clear, timely reports using structures such as **SBAR** (situation, background, assessment, recommendation), ask questions when instructions are unclear and close the loop by confirming tasks are done. Professionalism includes punctuality, appropriate attire, accepting feedback, respecting boundaries and continuing education.',
kase('An unclear request',{a:{text:'A nurse asks you to “take care of the IV in room 12” without saying what to do. What is the best response?',choices:[['Ask the nurse to clarify the specific task and check the order','b','Correct. Clarify before acting.',true],['Remove the IV to save time','b','Acting without clear direction or an order risks harm.',false]]},b:{text:'The nurse says the order is to discontinue the peripheral IV and the patient has no bleeding issues. What do you verify first?',choices:[['The physician’s order and any bleeding risk in the chart','c','Correct. This is the first step of Skill I.',true],['The patient’s meal preference','c','Start with the order and safety review.',false]]},c:{text:'You verify the order, gather supplies and discontinue the IV safely, then document and report to the nurse.',end:true}})]],
['Career pathways',[
'Patient care technician programs range from a few months to a year and often combine nursing assistant training with phlebotomy and EKG coursework, clinical externships and certification exams. Many PCTs use the role as a step toward nursing, medical school, physician assistant or other health careers, gaining hands-on experience and strong patient-care skills. Employment of nursing assistants and orderlies—the broader category that includes many PCT roles—is projected by the Bureau of Labor Statistics to grow as the population ages.',
'Related certifications include **phlebotomy technician** and **EKG technician** credentials, **CPR/BLS** certification (usually required) and specialty training such as **dialysis technician** certification. Strong PCTs are observant, compassionate, physically capable, organized and able to work calmly in fast-paced settings.',
flip('PCT credentials and skills',[['CPCT/A','NHA certification for patient care technicians/assistants'],['BLS','CPR certification required by most employers'],['Phlebotomy','Venipuncture and capillary collection'],['EKG','Recording 12-lead tracings and telemetry'],['Dialysis technician','Specialty role in kidney care'],['Externship','Supervised clinical training in a facility']])]]
],takeaways:['Test plan: patient care 44%, compliance/safety/professional responsibility 20%, phlebotomy 14%, infection control 12%, EKG 10%; seven Round Two skills need 70% combined.','PCTs work under RN supervision—ADLs, vitals, phlebotomy, EKG, glucose, catheter care, SCDs, IV discontinuation where allowed—but do not diagnose or give medications.','Use two identifiers (ID bracelet plus stated name and birthdate), obtain consent, protect HIPAA privacy, document objectively and report to the nurse.'],terms:[['Scope of practice','Duties a worker is trained and permitted to perform.'],['Two identifiers','Two pieces of information, such as name and birthdate, to confirm identity.'],['SBAR','Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation reporting tool.']],mcqs:[q('Which test area has the largest weight?','Patient care (44%)','EKG (10%)','Infection control (12%)','Phlebotomy (14%)','Patient care is nearly half the test.'),q('Which organization sponsors the PCT event?','National Healthcareer Association','American Heart Association','American Red Cross','AAMA','NHA offers the CPCT/A.'),q('How does HOSA require patients to be identified in PCT skills?','Check the ID bracelet and ask the patient to state name and birthdate','Use the room number','Ask “Are you John?”','Ask a visitor','Two identifiers are required.'),q('Which task is outside a PCT’s scope?','Interpreting an EKG to make a diagnosis','Recording a 12-lead EKG','Checking glucose','Applying SCDs','Diagnosis belongs to providers.'),q('Drawing blood after a patient refuses is:','Battery','Implied consent','Veracity','Justice','Refusals must be respected.'),q('Who supervises a PCT?','A registered nurse','A dietitian','A visitor','Another PCT only','Delegation comes from the RN.'),q('Competitors must bring how many pairs of non-latex gloves?','Two','One','Five','None','Along with a gown, eye protection and mask.'),q('Which reporting tool organizes communication with the nurse?','SBAR','ABCD','RACE','PASS','Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the role and scope of a patient care technician and describe the legal, privacy and communication practices that keep patients safe.',rubric:[['Role and duties',['adl','vital','phlebotomy','ekg','glucose','catheter','sequential compression']],['Scope limits',['nurse','supervision','diagnose','medication','policy','state']],['Legal and privacy',['consent','refuse','hipaa','identif','two identifiers','document']],['Communication',['explain','introduce','sbar','report','interpreter','listen']]],model:'Patient care technicians work under the supervision of registered nurses to provide personal care and activities of daily living, measure vital signs and intake and output, collect specimens, perform venipuncture, record 12-lead EKGs, check capillary blood glucose, maintain urinary catheters, apply sequential compression devices and, where policy allows, discontinue peripheral IVs, while observing and reporting changes. Their scope depends on state law, facility policy and training; they do not diagnose, interpret tests or give medications, and they decline tasks they are not trained for and notify the nurse. To keep patients safe, PCTs identify each patient with two identifiers—checking the ID bracelet and asking the patient to state their name and birthdate—explain procedures and obtain consent, respect refusals, protect privacy under HIPAA and document objectively and promptly. They introduce themselves, explain each step in plain language, use interpreters when needed, listen actively and report findings to the nurse using SBAR, confirming that tasks are completed.'},sources:[RULES,NHA,HIPAA,JC_NPSG]};

export const P2={title:'Patient safety, mobility and transport',hook:'When applying a splint to an extremity, where do you begin? At the distal end, working toward the body. Safety questions like this—along with falls, restraints, fire, emergencies and safe transfers—make up much of the 20% compliance and safety domain and protect patients every shift.',objectives:['Prevent falls and use restraint alternatives safely.','Respond to fire and medical emergencies.','Use body mechanics and safe patient handling for transfers and transport.','Apply splints and immobilization principles.'],sections:[
['Fall prevention and restraints',[
'Falls are among the most common hospital injuries. Risk factors include age, previous falls, weakness, dizziness, sedating medications, confusion, urgency to toilet, IV poles and tubing, and unfamiliar surroundings. Prevention measures include **bed in the lowest position with wheels locked**, **call light within reach**, hourly rounding (pain, position, possessions, potty), **nonskid footwear**, clear pathways, night lighting, bed or chair alarms for high-risk patients and assisting with toileting. If a patient begins to fall, ease them to the floor protecting the head, call for help and do not move them until assessed; report and document every fall.',
'**Restraints**—physical devices or medications that restrict movement—require a provider order, are used only as a last resort when alternatives fail and must be the least restrictive option. Patients in restraints are monitored frequently according to policy (often every 15 minutes), released and repositioned regularly, offered fluids and toileting and assessed for circulation and skin injury. Restraints are tied with quick-release knots to the bed frame, never the side rails.',
sort('Prevents falls or increases risk?',['Prevents falls','Increases risk'],[['Bed in the lowest position, wheels locked',0],['Call light within reach',0],['Hourly rounding',0],['Socks without nonskid soles',1],['Cluttered path to the bathroom',1],['Leaving a confused patient unattended on a commode',1]])]],
['Fire and emergency response',[
'For fire, follow **RACE**—**R**escue patients in immediate danger, **A**ctivate the alarm, **C**ontain the fire by closing doors, **E**xtinguish if small and safe or evacuate—and operate an extinguisher with **PASS**: **P**ull the pin, **A**im at the base, **S**queeze, **S**weep. Oxygen supports combustion, so keep it away from flames. Evacuate horizontally first (to another smoke compartment), then vertically if needed, starting with patients in immediate danger, then ambulatory patients, then those needing assistance.',
'For medical emergencies, call for help using the facility’s rapid response or code system, begin **CPR** with an AED if a patient is unresponsive and not breathing normally (as trained), and stay with the patient. Know emergency codes, the location of crash carts, oxygen and suction, and evacuation routes. Report changes such as chest pain, difficulty breathing, new confusion or signs of stroke immediately.',
flip('Safety acronyms',[['RACE','Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish or evacuate'],['PASS','Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep'],['BE FAST','Balance, Eyes, Face, Arm, Speech, Time—stroke'],['4 Ps of rounding','Pain, position, possessions, potty'],['Rapid response','Team called for a deteriorating patient'],['Horizontal evacuation','Move to another smoke compartment on the same floor']])]],
['Body mechanics and safe patient handling',[
'Lifting and moving patients causes many healthcare worker injuries. **Body mechanics**: wide base of support, bend at the hips and knees, keep the back straight, hold loads close, avoid twisting (pivot the feet), push rather than pull and raise the bed to working height. **Safe patient handling** programs use mechanical lifts, slide sheets, transfer boards and lift teams; **never lift a patient alone** when policy calls for equipment or help.',
'Common transfers include **moving up in bed** with a draw sheet and two caregivers, **stand-pivot transfers** from bed to chair (wheelchair on the patient’s strong side, wheels locked, gait belt applied), **sliding-board transfers** and **mechanical lift transfers**. Always explain, check orders for activity and weight-bearing limits, lower the bed and lock wheels, observe for dizziness after the patient sits up and keep tubes and lines free.',
order('Stand-pivot transfer from bed to wheelchair',['Explain and check activity orders','Place the wheelchair on the strong side; lock wheels; lift footrests','Lower and lock the bed; help the patient dangle; watch for dizziness','Apply a gait belt and nonskid footwear','Stand in front, brace the knees and lift on a count of three','Pivot to the chair and lower the patient','Position footrests, remove the belt and check comfort'])]],
['Transporting patients',[
'PCTs often transport patients by **wheelchair**, **stretcher** or **bed**. Before moving, verify the destination and identity, check that IV lines, catheters and oxygen are secured and adequate for the trip, and bring the chart or required documents. With wheelchairs, lock brakes during transfers, position the patient’s feet on footrests and **back into elevators and down ramps** so the patient faces away from the slope. With stretchers, raise side rails, fasten safety straps, move **feet first** in hallways (so the patient sees where they are going) and enter elevators **head first**. Never leave patients unattended in hallways, and communicate with the receiving department.',
'Monitor the patient during transport for changes in color, breathing or comfort, protect privacy with blankets and stop if lines become caught. After arrival, hand off to the receiving staff with a brief report.',
match('Match the transport rule to the equipment',[['Back into elevators','Wheelchair'],['Move feet first down hallways','Stretcher'],['Raise side rails and fasten straps','Stretcher'],['Lock brakes during transfers','Wheelchair'],['Go down ramps backward','Wheelchair'],['Secure IV pole and oxygen','Any transport']])]],
['Splints and immobilization',[
'Splints immobilize injured limbs to reduce pain and prevent further injury. A key principle: **begin at the distal part of the extremity and work toward the proximal part**, which supports venous return and helps prevent swelling trapped below the device. Immobilize the joints above and below a suspected fracture, pad bony areas and check **circulation, sensation and movement (CSM)**—color, warmth, capillary refill, pulses, numbness or tingling and the ability to move fingers or toes—**before and after** applying the splint. Report pain out of proportion, numbness, pallor or coldness immediately, as these may signal compromised circulation or compartment syndrome.',
'The same distal-to-proximal principle applies to elastic bandages and compression wraps. Sequential compression devices, covered in the next lesson, also push blood from distal to proximal to prevent clots.',
chk('When applying a splint to an extremity, where should you begin?',['At the distal part and work toward the proximal part','At the proximal part and work distally','At the midpoint and work outward','At the joint closest to the torso'],'A HOSA sample question; distal to proximal supports circulation.')]],
['Applying safety judgment',[
kase('A confused patient',{a:{text:'An 80-year-old patient with new confusion keeps trying to climb out of bed. What is the best first step?',choices:[['Use alternatives: stay with the patient, toilet, reorient, use a bed alarm and notify the nurse','b','Correct. Restraints are a last resort; confusion may signal a medical change.',true],['Tie the patient’s wrists to the side rails','b','Restraints require an order and are never tied to side rails.',false]]},b:{text:'The nurse obtains an order for a soft restraint after alternatives fail. How is it applied safely?',choices:[['Quick-release knot to the bed frame, check circulation and skin frequently and release regularly','c','Correct. Follow policy for monitoring and release.',true],['Tight knot to the side rail','c','Side rails move and can injure the patient.',false]]},c:{text:'The patient is monitored per policy, and the team investigates the cause of the confusion.',end:true}})]],
["Common bedside emergencies",["PCTs are often first to notice a patient in trouble. For **choking** in a conscious adult who cannot speak or cough, call for help and give **abdominal thrusts** until the object comes out or the patient becomes unresponsive, then lower them to the floor and begin **CPR** as trained. During a **seizure**, protect the patient from injury, pad the side rails if ordered, turn them on their side if possible, never restrain them or put anything in the mouth, time the seizure and notify the nurse. For suspected **hypoglycemia** (shaky, sweaty, confused), check glucose if ordered and notify the nurse immediately. For **fainting**, lower the patient safely, elevate the legs if appropriate and stay with them.","For **cardiac arrest**, check responsiveness and breathing, call the code according to policy, start **high-quality chest compressions** (at least 2 inches deep at 100–120 per minute for adults, allowing full recoil) and use an **AED** as soon as it arrives. Stay calm, follow the team leader’s directions and document what you observed and did once the patient is stabilized.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the emergency to the first action","pairs":[["Conscious adult choking","Abdominal thrusts"],["Seizure","Protect from injury; nothing in the mouth"],["Unresponsive, not breathing normally","Call for help and start CPR"],["Shaky, sweaty and confused","Notify the nurse; check glucose if ordered"],["Patient feels faint while standing","Lower safely and stay with them"],["AED arrives","Turn it on and follow the prompts"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Prevent falls: low locked bed, call light, hourly rounding, nonskid footwear; restraints are a last resort, ordered, tied to the bed frame with quick-release knots.','Fire: RACE and PASS; emergencies: call for help and start CPR as trained; body mechanics and lift equipment protect staff.','Transport: wheelchairs back into elevators and down ramps; stretchers feet first in halls; splint from distal to proximal and check CSM before and after.'],terms:[['Distal','Farther from the trunk or point of attachment.'],['Proximal','Closer to the trunk or point of attachment.'],['CSM check','Circulation, sensation and movement assessment.']],mcqs:[q('When applying a splint, you should begin at the:','Distal part and work toward the proximal part','Proximal part and work distally','Midpoint','Joint closest to the torso','A HOSA sample question.'),q('In RACE, “C” stands for:','Contain','Call','Clear','Carry','Close doors to contain fire.'),q('Restraints should be tied to the:','Bed frame with a quick-release knot','Side rail','Mattress','IV pole','Side rails move.'),q('When taking a wheelchair down a ramp, the PCT should:','Go backward so the patient faces up the ramp','Go forward quickly','Let the patient roll alone','Remove the footrests','This prevents the patient falling forward.'),q('A stretcher is moved through hallways:','Feet first','Head first','Sideways','Backward without looking','The patient sees where they are going.'),q('Which is part of hourly rounding?','Pain, position, possessions and potty','Changing all linens','Taking a full history','Drawing blood','It reduces falls and call lights.'),q('After applying a splint, you should check:','Circulation, sensation and movement','Only the patient’s name','Blood glucose','Nothing','Compare with before application.'),q('The first priority if a patient begins to fall is to:','Ease them to the floor, protecting the head','Hold them upright at all costs','Let go','Lift them back to bed alone','Do not fight the fall.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how a PCT keeps a high-fall-risk patient safe during a shift, including transferring and transporting the patient and responding if the patient falls.',rubric:[['Fall prevention',['low','lock','call light','rounding','nonskid','alarm']],['Safe transfer',['gait belt','strong side','lock','dangle','dizz','pivot']],['Transport',['wheelchair','back','elevator','ramp','secure','line']],['Response to a fall',['ease','floor','head','call','do not move','report','document']]],model:'I would keep the bed in the lowest position with the wheels locked, the call light and belongings within reach and the path to the bathroom clear and lit, make sure the patient wears nonskid footwear, round hourly for pain, position, possessions and toileting and use a bed alarm if ordered. For transfers, I would check activity orders, place the wheelchair on the patient’s strong side with brakes locked and footrests up, lower and lock the bed, help the patient dangle while watching for dizziness, apply a gait belt and pivot the patient to the chair using good body mechanics. During transport, I would secure IV lines and oxygen, keep the patient’s feet on the footrests, back into elevators and down ramps and never leave the patient unattended. If the patient began to fall, I would ease them to the floor protecting the head, call for help, keep them still until the nurse assesses for injury, then help as directed and document and report the fall according to policy.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_STEADI,OSHA_SPH]};

export const P3={title:'Infection control and PPE',hook:'Which piece of personal protective equipment comes off first? The gloves—they are the most contaminated. Infection control is 12% of the test and part of every skill, which all end with hand hygiene and “practiced standard precautions throughout.”',objectives:['Explain the chain of infection and healthcare-associated infections.','Apply standard and transmission-based precautions.','Don and remove PPE in the correct order.','Handle sharps, specimens, linens and spills safely.'],sections:[
['The chain of infection and HAIs',[
'Infection requires an **infectious agent**, a **reservoir**, a **portal of exit**, a **mode of transmission** (contact, droplet, airborne, vehicle or vector), a **portal of entry** and a **susceptible host**. Hospital patients are especially susceptible because of illness, surgery, invasive devices and medications. **Healthcare-associated infections (HAIs)** include **catheter-associated urinary tract infections (CAUTI)**, **central line–associated bloodstream infections (CLABSI)**, **surgical site infections**, **ventilator-associated pneumonia** and ***Clostridioides difficile*** infection. Many are preventable with consistent hand hygiene, device care and cleaning.',
'PCTs break the chain by hand hygiene, using PPE, cleaning equipment between patients, maintaining closed catheter systems, keeping drainage bags below the bladder, handling linens properly and staying home when ill. Signs of infection to report include fever, redness, swelling, warmth, drainage, cough, cloudy urine and new confusion in older adults.',
order('Arrange the chain of infection',['Infectious agent','Reservoir','Portal of exit','Mode of transmission','Portal of entry','Susceptible host'])]],
['Hand hygiene',[
'Hand hygiene is the single most important infection-prevention measure. Use **alcohol-based hand rub** for most situations—rubbing all surfaces until dry—and **soap and water** when hands are visibly soiled, after caring for patients with *C. difficile* or norovirus, and before eating. The five moments for hand hygiene are **before touching a patient**, **before a clean or aseptic procedure**, **after body fluid exposure risk**, **after touching a patient** and **after touching patient surroundings**. Remove gloves and perform hand hygiene between patients and between dirty and clean tasks on the same patient.',
'Keep nails short and natural, avoid rings and wrist jewelry during patient care and use lotion to prevent skin cracks. HOSA skills award points for hand hygiene at the start and end and after removing gloves.',
sort('Soap and water or alcohol rub?',['Soap and water required','Alcohol rub is appropriate'],[['Hands visibly soiled with blood',0],['After caring for a patient with C. difficile diarrhea',0],['Before taking routine vital signs',1],['After removing gloves following a blood draw (no visible soil)',1],['Before eating lunch',0],['Entering a room to deliver a meal tray',1]])]],
['Standard and transmission-based precautions',[
'**Standard precautions** apply to every patient: treat blood, body fluids (except sweat), non-intact skin and mucous membranes as potentially infectious; perform hand hygiene; wear gloves when contact is expected and gown, mask and eye protection for splashes; follow respiratory hygiene; handle sharps safely; and clean equipment. **Transmission-based precautions** are added for known or suspected infections: **contact** (gown and gloves—MRSA, VRE, *C. difficile*, scabies), **droplet** (surgical mask within close range—influenza, pertussis, some meningitis) and **airborne** (fit-tested N95 respirator and airborne infection isolation room with negative pressure—tuberculosis, measles, varicella).',
'Patients on precautions have signs at the door listing required PPE; dedicated equipment such as stethoscopes and blood pressure cuffs stays in the room when possible. Transport of patients on airborne or droplet precautions requires a mask on the patient.',
sort('Which precaution category?',['Contact','Droplet','Airborne'],[['MRSA wound',0],['C. difficile',0],['Influenza',1],['Pertussis',1],['Tuberculosis',2],['Measles',2]])]],
['Donning and removing PPE',[
'**Donning** (putting on) order: **gown** (tied at neck and waist, covering torso and arms), **mask or respirator** (fitted to the nose and under the chin; N95 seal checked), **goggles or face shield**, then **gloves** pulled over the gown cuffs. **Removing (doffing)** order follows the CDC sequence: **gloves first** (most contaminated), then **goggles or face shield** (handle by the ear pieces or headband), then **gown** (pull away from the body, roll inside out), then **mask or respirator** (handle by the ties or elastic; do not touch the front), and **hand hygiene** immediately. Some facilities remove the gown and gloves together. Remove PPE at the doorway or anteroom—respirators after leaving the room.',
'Never touch your face with contaminated gloves, change gloves if torn, and perform hand hygiene whenever hands may have become contaminated during removal.',
order('Remove PPE (CDC sequence)',['Remove gloves','Remove goggles or face shield','Remove the gown, rolling it inside out','Remove the mask or respirator by the ties','Perform hand hygiene']),
chk('Which piece of PPE should a patient care technician remove first?',['Gloves','Face shield','Mask','Goggles'],'A HOSA sample question—gloves are the most contaminated.')]],
['Sharps, specimens, linens and spills',[
'The **OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard** requires employers to provide PPE, offer the **hepatitis B vaccine**, use engineering controls such as **safety needles** and **sharps containers**, train employees and provide post-exposure follow-up. PCTs **activate safety devices immediately**, **never recap used needles**, dispose of sharps at the point of use in containers that are not overfilled (usually replaced at three-quarters full) and report any needlestick or splash immediately after washing the area.',
'Specimens go in sealed, labeled containers inside biohazard bags. Soiled **linens** are held away from the uniform, never shaken, rolled with the soiled side in and placed in the hamper. **Spills** of blood or body fluids are contained, cleaned with gloves and absorbent material and disinfected with an approved disinfectant following contact time. Equipment such as glucometers and EKG machines is cleaned between patients according to policy.',
kase('A needlestick',{a:{text:'After a venipuncture, you feel a needle prick through your glove before you engaged the safety device. What should you do first?',choices:[['Wash the area with soap and water and report the exposure immediately','b','Correct. Prompt washing and reporting allow post-exposure evaluation.',true],['Finish your shift and report tomorrow','b','Post-exposure treatment may be time-sensitive.',false]]},b:{text:'What could have prevented the injury?',choices:[['Engaging the safety device immediately and disposing of the unit in the sharps container','c','Correct. Activate safety devices right away; never recap.',true],['Recapping the needle with two hands','c','Recapping causes needlesticks.',false]]},c:{text:'You complete the exposure report and receive evaluation under the facility’s exposure control plan.',end:true}})]],
["Cleaning, disinfection and isolation rooms",["Equipment is processed according to risk. **Noncritical** items that touch intact skin—blood pressure cuffs, stethoscopes, thermometers, glucometers, bed rails—are cleaned and disinfected between patients with an approved disinfectant, leaving the surface wet for the product’s **contact (dwell) time**. **Semicritical** items that touch mucous membranes need high-level disinfection, and **critical** items that enter sterile tissue must be **sterilized**; PCTs return reusable instruments to sterile processing rather than cleaning them at the bedside. Cleaning removes soil first, because dirt protects microorganisms from disinfectants. Use bleach-based products for *C. difficile* rooms as directed.","In **isolation rooms**, gather supplies before entering so you minimize trips, take in only what is needed, leave dedicated equipment in the room, keep doors closed for airborne precautions and place the patient’s specimens in sealed bags at the door. Patients in isolation may feel lonely, so explain the reason for precautions, check on them regularly and keep the call light within reach.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Which processing level?","buckets":["Clean and disinfect between patients (noncritical)","Send for sterilization (critical)"],"items":[["Blood pressure cuff",0],["Glucometer",0],["Surgical scissors",1],["Stethoscope",0],["Suture removal kit instruments (reusable)",1],["Bed rails",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Break the chain of infection; HAIs such as CAUTI, CLABSI and C. difficile are often preventable.','Hand hygiene at the five moments; soap and water for visible soil and C. difficile; standard precautions for all, plus contact, droplet or airborne precautions.','Don gown, mask, eye protection, gloves; remove gloves first, then eye protection, gown, mask, hand hygiene; never recap needles; report exposures immediately.'],terms:[['Healthcare-associated infection','Infection acquired while receiving care.'],['Standard precautions','Infection-control practices used for every patient.'],['Airborne infection isolation room','Negative-pressure room for airborne diseases.']],mcqs:[q('Which PPE should be removed first?','Gloves','Face shield','Mask','Goggles','A HOSA sample question.'),q('Which PPE is put on first?','Gown','Gloves','Goggles','Mask','Gloves go on last.'),q('Which infection requires an N95 respirator and a negative-pressure room?','Tuberculosis','MRSA wound','Influenza','C. difficile','Airborne precautions.'),q('Hands must be washed with soap and water after caring for a patient with:','C. difficile','A cold with no symptoms','A broken arm','High blood pressure','Alcohol does not kill the spores.'),q('Used needles should be:','Disposed of immediately in a sharps container without recapping','Recapped with two hands','Placed in regular trash','Left on the tray','Activate the safety device first.'),q('Which is a healthcare-associated infection?','Catheter-associated urinary tract infection','Seasonal allergies','A fracture','Hypertension','CAUTI is preventable.'),q('Soiled linens should be:','Rolled with the soiled side in and held away from the uniform','Shaken out','Placed on the floor','Carried against the body','Prevents spreading microorganisms.'),q('Droplet precautions require:','A surgical mask within close range','An N95 and negative pressure','No PPE','Only gloves','For influenza and pertussis.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how a PCT prevents the spread of infection when caring for a patient on contact precautions for MRSA who needs a blood draw.',rubric:[['Chain and precautions',['contact','mrsa','standard precautions','chain','gown','gloves']],['Donning and doffing',['gown','gloves','remove','first','inside out','hand hygiene']],['Sharps and specimens',['safety device','sharps','recap','label','biohazard']],['Cleaning and reporting',['clean','disinfect','dedicated','equipment','report','exposure']]],model:'MRSA spreads by contact with the patient and their environment, so in addition to standard precautions I would follow contact precautions shown on the door sign. I would perform hand hygiene, put on a gown tied at the neck and waist and gloves pulled over the cuffs, plus eye protection and a mask if splashes were possible, and use dedicated equipment kept in the room. For the blood draw, I would identify the patient with two identifiers, perform the venipuncture, activate the needle safety device immediately and dispose of the unit in the sharps container without recapping, then label the tubes at the bedside and place them in a sealed biohazard bag. Before leaving, I would clean shared surfaces with an approved disinfectant, remove my gloves first, then eye protection, then roll the gown inside out and remove the mask by its ties at the doorway, and perform hand hygiene immediately. If any needlestick or splash occurred, I would wash the area and report it right away.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HH,CDC_ISO,OSHA_BBP]};

export const P4={title:'Vital signs and measurements, including infant length and weight (Skill III)',hook:'Patient care is 44% of the test, and measurements are its foundation. A PCT measures vital signs dozens of times a shift, weighs patients with heart failure, and in pediatric settings measures infants’ length and weight—Skill III—to track growth on standardized charts.',objectives:['Measure temperature, pulse, respirations, blood pressure, oxygen saturation and pain accurately.','Recognize abnormal adult and pediatric values and report them.','Measure an infant’s length and weight following HOSA Skill III.','Plot measurements on growth charts.'],sections:[
['Vital signs in adults',[
'Adult teaching ranges: **temperature** about 97.6–99.6°F orally (tympanic and rectal slightly higher, axillary lower); **pulse 60–100** beats per minute; **respirations 12–20** per minute; **blood pressure** below **120/80 mm Hg** normal, with hypertension beginning at 130/80; and **oxygen saturation** about 95–100% in healthy adults. **Pain** is assessed with a 0–10 scale or faces scale. Measure pulse and respirations for a full minute when irregular, count respirations without the patient’s awareness and use the correct blood pressure cuff size (too small reads high, too large reads low).',
'Always compare with the patient’s **baseline** and any parameters in the care plan. Report immediately: new chest pain, difficulty breathing, oxygen saturation below the ordered limit, very high or low blood pressure, a pulse that is new, irregular, below 50 or above 120 (or outside facility parameters), fever, and changes in level of consciousness.',
{t:'w',kind:'vitalsreport',title:'Report or record?',prompt:'Decide which adult readings fall within the usual teaching ranges and which you would report to the nurse.',person:'patient',reportTo:'nurse'}]],
['Pediatric vital signs and measurements',[
'Children have faster heart and breathing rates and lower blood pressures than adults. Approximate ranges: **newborns**—heart rate about 100–160 and respirations about 30–60; **toddlers**—heart rate about 90–150 and respirations about 24–40; **school-age children**—heart rate about 70–120 and respirations about 18–30. Infants are weighed **without clothing or diaper** on an infant scale, and length is measured lying down until about age 2; older children are measured standing. **Head circumference** is measured at well-child visits, usually until about age 2–3.',
'Measurements are plotted on **growth charts**—the CDC recommends **WHO growth charts for children under 2** and **CDC growth charts from age 2**—to compare with percentiles and, more importantly, to follow a child’s trend over time. A child crossing two major percentile lines or falling below the 5th percentile needs follow-up.',
match('Match the age group to typical resting heart rate',[['Newborn','About 100–160'],['Toddler','About 90–150'],['School-age child','About 70–120'],['Adult','About 60–100'],['Newborn respirations','About 30–60'],['Adult respirations','About 12–20']])]],
['Measuring an infant’s length and weight (Skill III)',[
'In Skill III (7 minutes), the competitor assembles supplies, performs hand hygiene, **identifies the patient by the ID bracelet and asks the caregiver to state the infant’s name and birthdate**, introduces themselves and explains the skill to the caregiver, **prepares the scale by lining it with disposable paper** and provides privacy. For **length**, the caregiver places the infant **on the back on the examination paper**; the infant is undressed (the **diaper may stay on** for length); the caregiver **gently holds the head still**; the competitor **straightens one leg**, **marks the paper at the top of the head and at the heel** after the leg is extended, and **measures between the marks with a tape measure** and records it. For **weight**, the competitor **removes the diaper**, **places the infant gently on the scale while keeping a hand above the infant’s trunk for safety** and records the weight.',
'The competitor then performs hand hygiene, **records length and weight in the Nurses’ Notes** and **completes the growth chart for both measurements by connecting the dots from the last visit**, with appropriate communication and standard precautions (33 points; 70% is 23.1). Never leave an infant unattended on a scale or table.',
order('Infant length and weight (HOSA Skill III)',['Identify the infant with the caregiver; explain; line the scale with paper','Place the infant on the back on the exam paper; diaper may stay on','Caregiver holds the head still; straighten one leg','Mark the paper at the top of the head and at the heel','Measure between the marks and record the length','Remove the diaper and place the infant on the scale, hand hovering above the trunk','Record the weight','Record in the Nurses’ Notes and plot both on the growth chart'])]],
['Height, weight and other measurements in adults',[
'Adults are weighed at the **same time of day, on the same scale, in similar clothing**—often in the morning after voiding—because daily weights detect fluid retention in heart failure and kidney disease; a gain of about 2–3 pounds in a day or 5 pounds in a week is commonly reported. Bed scales and chair scales are used for patients who cannot stand. Height is measured standing against a wall-mounted device or, for bedbound patients, lying flat. **Body mass index** is calculated from height and weight.',
'**Pulse oximetry** estimates oxygen saturation using a sensor on a finger or earlobe; cold extremities, nail polish, movement and poor circulation can cause inaccurate readings. **Intake and output** are recorded in milliliters (1 ounce = 30 mL). **Pain** scales include numeric (0–10), Wong-Baker FACES for children and behavioral scales for patients who cannot self-report.',
{t:'w',kind:'iolab',title:'Total intake and output',prompt:'Convert ounces to millilitres and round output readings between lines up to the nearest 25 mL.'}]],
['Accuracy and reporting',[
'Accuracy matters: record the site for temperatures, note the position for blood pressure, use calibrated equipment and recheck values that seem wrong. Document each value with the time on the graphic sheet or EHR and report abnormal values to the nurse promptly, with context (“BP 182/104, patient reports headache”). For infants, double-check that the scale was zeroed with the paper in place and that marks were made at the exact top of the head and heel.',
kase('A weight concern',{a:{text:'An infant weighed 7 lb 4 oz at the last visit and plots at the 50th percentile. Today, after the measurement, the infant plots at the 10th percentile. What should you do?',choices:[['Recheck the scale and technique, re-weigh, then report the change to the nurse','b','Correct. Verify accuracy, then report a significant change.',true],['Record it without telling anyone','b','Crossing percentile lines needs follow-up.',false]]},b:{text:'You find the diaper was removed today but was on at the last weight. What do you document?',choices:[['The accurate weight today and a note about the measurement conditions','c','Correct. Consistent technique and clear documentation matter.',true],['Change last visit’s weight','c','Never alter previous entries.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse reviews the growth trend and feeding history with the caregiver.',end:true}})]],
["Pain and orthostatic vital signs",["**Pain** is assessed regularly and whenever a patient reports discomfort. Ask about **location, intensity** (0–10 or a faces scale), **quality** (sharp, dull, burning), **onset and duration** and what makes it better or worse. Watch for **nonverbal signs**—grimacing, guarding, moaning, restlessness or changes in behavior—especially in patients who cannot report pain, such as those with dementia. PCTs do not give pain medication, but they report pain promptly, provide comfort measures allowed by policy (repositioning, pillows, a calm environment) and recheck after the nurse intervenes.","**Orthostatic vital signs** detect drops in blood pressure when patients change position, which can cause dizziness and falls. Typically, blood pressure and pulse are measured after the patient lies down for a few minutes, then again after sitting and standing (usually at 1 and 3 minutes). A drop of **20 mm Hg or more in systolic pressure** or **10 mm Hg or more in diastolic pressure**, or symptoms such as dizziness, are reported. Stay close and use a gait belt because the patient may become lightheaded.",{"t":"example","title":"Recognize orthostatic hypotension","prompt":"A patient’s blood pressure is 132/78 lying down and 108/70 one minute after standing, and she feels lightheaded. Is this significant?","steps":["Systolic change: 132 − 108 = 24 mm Hg.","A drop of 20 mm Hg or more in systolic pressure is significant.","She also has symptoms."],"answer":"Yes. Help her sit or lie down safely, then report the readings and symptoms to the nurse. (Values are invented for practice.)"}]]
],takeaways:['Adult teaching ranges: pulse 60–100, respirations 12–20, BP below 120/80, SpO₂ about 95–100%; compare with baseline and report changes.','Children have faster heart and breathing rates; use WHO charts under 2 and CDC charts from 2; follow trends over time.','Skill III: line the scale, infant supine on paper, caregiver holds head, straighten one leg, mark head and heel, measure; remove diaper, hand above trunk on the scale; record and plot both by connecting dots from last visit.'],terms:[['Baseline','A patient’s usual values used for comparison.'],['Growth chart','Graph comparing a child’s measurements with percentiles.'],['Pulse oximetry','Noninvasive estimate of oxygen saturation.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill III, the scale is prepared by:','Lining it with disposable paper','Covering it with a blanket','Adding a pillow','Nothing','Zero the scale with paper in place.'),q('For the length measurement, the infant may keep on:','The diaper','A full outfit','Shoes','A blanket','The diaper is removed for weight.'),q('While the infant is on the scale, the competitor should:','Keep a hand above the infant’s trunk for safety','Step away to record','Hold the infant’s legs','Leave the room','Never leave an infant unattended.'),q('Length is measured by marking the paper at:','The top of the head and the heel with the leg extended','The shoulders and knees','The chin and toes with legs bent','The ears and hips','Then measure between the marks.'),q('Which growth charts are recommended for children under 2?','WHO growth charts','Adult BMI charts','CDC charts only','No charts','CDC charts are used from age 2.'),q('A typical newborn resting heart rate is about:','100–160','40–60','60–80','180–220','Children’s rates are faster.'),q('Daily weights should be taken:','At the same time, on the same scale, in similar clothing','At random times','On different scales','After meals only','Consistency shows trends.'),q('A blood pressure cuff that is too small gives a reading that is:','Falsely high','Falsely low','Accurate','Unaffected','Use the correct size.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to measure and record an infant’s length and weight following HOSA Skill III, and explain how growth charts are used and when to report findings.',rubric:[['Preparation and identification',['identif','caregiver','birthdate','explain','paper','privacy']],['Length technique',['back','head still','straighten','mark','heel','tape measure']],['Weight technique and safety',['diaper','scale','hand above','trunk','record']],['Growth charts and reporting',['growth chart','connect','percentile','trend','who','report','nurse']]],model:'I would gather supplies, perform hand hygiene, identify the infant by the ID bracelet and ask the caregiver to state the infant’s name and birthdate, introduce myself, explain the procedure, line the scale with disposable paper and provide privacy. For length, the caregiver places the infant on the back on the exam paper, the infant is undressed except for the diaper and the caregiver gently holds the head still; I straighten one leg, mark the paper at the top of the head and at the heel, then measure between the marks with a tape measure and record the length. For weight, I remove the diaper, place the infant gently on the scale while keeping my hand above the trunk for safety and record the weight. After hand hygiene, I record both measurements in the Nurses’ Notes and plot them on the growth chart—WHO charts for children under 2—connecting the dots from the last visit. Growth charts show percentiles and, more importantly, the child’s trend; I would recheck and report to the nurse any measurement that crosses major percentile lines, falls below the 5th percentile or differs sharply from previous visits.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_VITALS,CDC_GROWTH]};

export const P5={title:'Mobility, skin integrity and sequential compression devices (Skill II)',hook:'Hospitalized patients who lie still are at risk of two silent dangers: pressure injuries and deep vein thrombosis. Repositioning, skin care and sequential compression devices—Skill II—are simple PCT tasks that prevent serious harm.',objectives:['Prevent pressure injuries through positioning and skin care.','Explain deep vein thrombosis and how prevention works.','Apply sequential compression devices following HOSA Skill II.','Assist with range of motion and ambulation safely.'],sections:[
['Pressure injuries',[
'**Pressure injuries** develop where sustained pressure, often with **shear** and **friction**, reduces blood flow to skin and tissue over **bony prominences**—the sacrum, coccyx, heels, hips, elbows, shoulder blades and back of the head. Stages range from **Stage 1** (intact skin with redness that does not blanch) through **Stage 2** (partial-thickness skin loss or blister), **Stage 3** (full-thickness skin loss into fat), **Stage 4** (exposed muscle, tendon or bone) to **unstageable** and **deep tissue** injuries. Risk factors include immobility, incontinence, poor nutrition, low blood pressure, sensory loss and medical devices pressing on skin.',
'Prevention: **reposition at least every 2 hours** in bed (more often if needed), use pressure-redistributing mattresses, **float the heels** with pillows under the calves, keep skin clean and dry, manage incontinence promptly, use lift sheets instead of dragging, keep the head of the bed at or below 30 degrees when possible to reduce shear, check skin under devices and encourage nutrition and fluids. Report any reddened area that does not fade.',
sort('Prevents pressure injuries or increases risk?',['Prevents','Increases risk'],[['Repositioning every 2 hours',0],['Floating the heels',0],['Dragging a patient up in bed',1],['Leaving a wet incontinence pad in place',1],['Keeping the head of bed at 30° or lower when allowed',0],['Massaging reddened bony areas',1]])]],
['Deep vein thrombosis and prevention',[
'**Deep vein thrombosis (DVT)** is a blood clot in a deep vein, usually in the leg. **Virchow’s triad** explains clot formation: **venous stasis** (slow blood flow from immobility), **vessel wall injury** (surgery or trauma) and **hypercoagulability** (cancer, pregnancy, estrogen, dehydration). A DVT can break loose and travel to the lungs as a **pulmonary embolism (PE)**, a life-threatening emergency with sudden shortness of breath, chest pain and rapid heart rate. DVT signs include one-sided leg swelling, warmth, redness and pain.',
'Prevention includes **early ambulation**, leg exercises, hydration, anticoagulant medications ordered by the provider, **graduated compression stockings** and **sequential compression devices (SCDs)**, which inflate and deflate sleeves around the legs to squeeze blood from distal to proximal and reduce stasis. PCTs never massage a leg suspected of having a DVT and report symptoms immediately.',
flip('DVT essentials',[['Venous stasis','Slow flow from immobility'],['Vessel injury','From surgery or trauma'],['Hypercoagulability','Increased clotting tendency'],['DVT signs','One-sided swelling, warmth, redness, pain'],['PE signs','Sudden shortness of breath and chest pain'],['SCDs','Inflate sequentially to move blood toward the heart']])]],
['Applying sequential compression devices (Skill II)',[
'In Skill II (6 minutes), the competitor assembles supplies, performs hand hygiene, **identifies the patient by the ID bracelet and stated name and birthdate**, explains the procedure and provides privacy. They **arrange the SCD sleeve under the patient’s leg according to the leg position marked on the inner lining**, lining up the **back of the patient’s ankle with the ankle marking** and positioning the **back of the knee at the popliteal opening**. They **wrap the sleeve securely**, **verify the fit by placing two fingers between the leg and the sleeve**, **attach the sleeve connector to the plug on the mechanical unit using the arrow on the plug**, **turn the unit on and verify it is functioning** and **observe one full cycle**. They reposition the patient for comfort, perform hand hygiene and document in the nursing notes, with communication and standard precautions (28 points; 70% is 19.6).',
'Additional practice points: check the skin under sleeves each shift, remove sleeves for bathing and ambulation as ordered, keep tubing free of kinks and report alarms, numbness or skin breakdown. SCDs are not applied to a leg with a known or suspected DVT unless ordered.',
order('Apply SCDs (HOSA Skill II)',['Identify the patient, explain and provide privacy','Place the sleeve under the leg per the inner-lining markings','Line up the ankle and position the knee at the popliteal opening','Wrap the sleeve securely','Check fit with two fingers between leg and sleeve','Connect the tubing to the unit using the arrow on the plug','Turn the unit on and observe one full cycle','Reposition for comfort, hand hygiene and document']),
chk('How is the fit of an SCD sleeve verified?',['Two fingers fit between the leg and the sleeve','The sleeve cannot move at all','A whole hand fits under the sleeve','By squeezing the patient’s calf'],'Too tight restricts circulation; too loose is ineffective.')]],
['Positioning and range of motion',[
'Positions include **supine**, **Fowler’s** (head of bed 45–60°), **semi-Fowler’s** (30–45°), **lateral** (side-lying with pillows supporting the back, between the knees and under the upper arm), **prone** and **Sims’**. Maintain **body alignment**, support joints and prevent **foot drop** with supports. **Range-of-motion (ROM)** exercises—active, active-assisted or passive—prevent contractures and maintain circulation; support the joint, move slowly and stop at resistance or pain.',
'Changing position also improves breathing and comfort. Patients who cannot reposition themselves depend on the PCT to follow a turning schedule documented in the chart.',
match('Match the position to its description',[['Fowler’s','Head of bed about 45–60°'],['Semi-Fowler’s','Head of bed about 30–45°'],['Lateral','Side-lying with pillow supports'],['Prone','Lying on the abdomen'],['Sims’','Left side with the right knee bent'],['Supine','Lying flat on the back']])]],
['Ambulation',[
'Walking prevents DVT, pressure injuries, pneumonia and deconditioning. Before ambulating, check orders and weight-bearing status, measure vital signs if indicated, apply a **gait belt** and **nonskid footwear**, manage lines and drainage bags (bags below the bladder), have the patient dangle first and walk on the patient’s **weaker side, slightly behind**, holding the gait belt. Watch for dizziness, pallor, shortness of breath or pain, and have a chair available. Canes are held on the strong side; walkers are moved first, then the patient steps in.',
kase('A swollen leg',{a:{text:'While applying SCDs, you notice the patient’s left calf is swollen, warm and tender compared with the right. What should you do?',choices:[['Do not apply the sleeve to that leg; report to the nurse immediately','b','Correct. These may be signs of DVT.',true],['Massage the calf to reduce swelling','b','Massage could dislodge a clot.',false]]},b:{text:'Later the patient suddenly becomes short of breath with chest pain. What is your action?',choices:[['Call for help immediately and stay with the patient','c','Correct. These are signs of a possible pulmonary embolism.',true],['Tell the patient to rest and check back in an hour','c','This is an emergency.',false]]},c:{text:'The rapid response team arrives. You give a brief report of the leg findings and the time symptoms began.',end:true}})]],
["Personal care, nutrition and feeding",["Personal care is a large part of patient care. **Bathing** (complete, partial or shower) cleans the skin, promotes circulation and allows skin inspection; wash from cleanest to dirtiest, check water temperature and keep the patient covered and warm. **Oral care** prevents pneumonia, especially for patients on ventilators or who cannot swallow; unconscious patients are turned on their side with minimal liquid used. **Perineal care**, **hair care**, **shaving** (electric razors for patients on anticoagulants), **nail care** (no toenail cutting for patients with diabetes) and **dressing** preserve dignity—dress the weak side first.","**Nutrition** supports healing. PCTs check diet orders and identification before serving trays, position patients **upright (high Fowler’s)** for meals, assist with feeding by offering small bites and allowing time to swallow, watch for coughing or choking in patients with **dysphagia** and follow **thickened-liquid** orders. They record the **percentage of meals eaten** and fluid intake and report poor appetite, nausea or difficulty swallowing. Patients who are **NPO** (nothing by mouth) before procedures receive no food or fluids.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Correct or incorrect?","buckets":["Correct","Incorrect"],"items":[["Positioning a patient upright for meals",0],["Serving a regular tray to an NPO patient",1],["Following a thickened-liquid order",0],["Using a straight razor on a patient taking warfarin",1],["Recording the percentage of a meal eaten",0],["Oral care with lots of water for an unconscious patient lying flat",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Prevent pressure injuries: reposition every 2 hours, float heels, keep skin clean and dry, lift instead of drag, report non-blanching redness.','DVT follows Virchow’s triad; signs are one-sided swelling, warmth and pain; PE causes sudden dyspnea and chest pain—report immediately; never massage.','Skill II: sleeve per inner-lining markings, ankle aligned and knee at the popliteal opening, wrap securely, two-finger fit, connect by the arrow, turn on and watch one full cycle, document.'],terms:[['Pressure injury','Damage to skin and tissue from sustained pressure.'],['Deep vein thrombosis','Blood clot in a deep vein.'],['Sequential compression device','Inflating leg sleeves that promote venous return.']],mcqs:[q('How is SCD fit checked?','Two fingers between the leg and sleeve','A fist under the sleeve','No space at all','By the patient’s report only','A Skill II step.'),q('In Skill II, the back of the knee is positioned at the:','Popliteal opening','Ankle marking','Thigh strap','Connector','Follow the inner-lining markings.'),q('After turning on the SCD unit, the competitor:','Observes one full cycle','Leaves immediately','Removes the sleeve','Turns it off','Verify it functions.'),q('Which is a sign of DVT?','One-sided leg swelling, warmth and pain','Bilateral cold feet only','A cough','A rash on the arm','Report it.'),q('Sudden shortness of breath and chest pain may indicate:','Pulmonary embolism','A pressure injury','Normal recovery','Dehydration only','This is an emergency.'),q('Pressure injuries most often occur over:','Bony prominences','The abdomen','The palms','The thighs only','Pressure reduces blood flow.'),q('A Stage 1 pressure injury is:','Intact skin with non-blanching redness','Exposed bone','A deep crater','Full-thickness loss','Report it promptly.'),q('Which is part of Virchow’s triad?','Venous stasis','Low cholesterol','High oxygen','Normal mobility','Plus vessel injury and hypercoagulability.')],frq:{prompt:'A postoperative patient is on bed rest. Explain how you would prevent pressure injuries and DVT, including applying SCDs following HOSA Skill II, and which findings you would report.',rubric:[['Pressure injury prevention',['reposition','2 hours','heel','dry','lift','bony']],['DVT understanding',['dvt','stasis','immobil','clot','pulmonary embolism']],['Skill II technique',['inner lining','ankle','popliteal','wrap','two fingers','arrow','cycle']],['Reporting',['swelling','warmth','pain','shortness of breath','chest pain','skin','report']]],model:'Because bed rest causes immobility, I would reposition the patient at least every two hours, float the heels on pillows, keep the skin clean and dry, use a lift sheet rather than dragging, check skin under devices and report any non-blanching redness. Immobility also slows venous blood flow, one part of Virchow’s triad along with surgical vessel injury and increased clotting, so the patient is at risk for DVT. To apply SCDs, I would identify the patient, explain and provide privacy, place each sleeve under the leg according to the inner-lining markings with the ankle aligned and the popliteal opening behind the knee, wrap it securely, check that two fingers fit between leg and sleeve, connect the tubing using the arrow on the plug, turn the unit on, observe one full cycle, reposition the patient and document. I would encourage leg exercises and ambulation as ordered. I would report immediately one-sided leg swelling, warmth, redness or pain (and not apply the sleeve or massage the leg), any skin breakdown under the sleeves and, as an emergency, sudden shortness of breath or chest pain that could signal a pulmonary embolism.'},sources:[RULES,AHRQ_PU,NHLBI_VTE]};
